import{_ as a,o as t,c as r,m as o}from"./chunks/framework.D3-xQjZb.js";const n="/images/editorial/evidence-desk.jpg",w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Rethinking Education with AI","description":"Explore Keith Williams’s research agenda in AI-assisted learning, evidence in software systems, and the human decisions behind reliable engineering.","frontmatter":{"title":"Rethinking Education with AI","description":"Explore Keith Williams’s research agenda in AI-assisted learning, evidence in software systems, and the human decisions behind reliable engineering.","teaser":"A teaching Studio and a video-editing prototype brought me to the same problem. When an AI helps produce an answer, how can someone independently check it?"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research.md","filePath":"research.md"}'),s={name:"research.md"};function i(h,e,c,d,l,u){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="rethinking-education-with-ai" tabindex="-1">Rethinking Education with AI <a class="header-anchor" href="#rethinking-education-with-ai" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rethinking Education with AI&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In my NJIT Studios, I can change one rule in a game and ask the person who built it which test should fail. In MetaVis, my Swift and Metal editing prototype, a reference frame can pass a pixel check while the exported video still needs a sound check. Both experiences led me to ask what a test actually lets another person conclude.</p><p>I’m pursuing that question in two directions. For instructors, I want to study which check a learner chooses first after the coach steps back. For teams using AI to prepare briefings, I want to study what it takes to trace a disputed sentence to its original source. These are proposed studies. You can examine the classroom starter and try a paper exercise that makes the second problem visible. <a href="/teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code">Read the classroom feature</a> · <a href="/knowledge-lab/guide/walkthrough" target="_self">Trace one claim across three reports</a>.</p><h2 id="can-you-use-what-you-learned-somewhere-new" tabindex="-1">Can you use what you learned somewhere new? <a class="header-anchor" href="#can-you-use-what-you-learned-somewhere-new" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can you use what you learned somewhere new?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In a classroom Studio, the AI coach can offer hints and help run checks. A follow-up problem gives the student a fresh choice. Which test would they run first? What evidence would make them change their mind?</p><p>I’d like to work with another instructor on a common follow-up problem after students have had different amounts of coaching. We could compare three observations from each attempt. Who suggested the first check? What did the learner predict? How did they explain the result? <a href="/teaching/studio-field-notes">Explore the research behind the Studio</a></p><h2 id="what-should-count-as-evidence-of-learning" tabindex="-1">What should count as evidence of learning? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-should-count-as-evidence-of-learning" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What should count as evidence of learning?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A student changes their mind halfway through a project. The final version may look much like a classmate’s, while the change itself tells a different story. I want to know whether another instructor can identify that decision from a selected exchange, an annotated draft, or a brief demonstration. I also want to know how much teaching time each approach takes.</p><h2 id="building-knowledge-a-reader-can-check" tabindex="-1">Building knowledge a reader can check <a class="header-anchor" href="#building-knowledge-a-reader-can-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Building knowledge a reader can check&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+n+'" alt="A magnifying glass enlarges a marked spot on layers of blue tracing paper."></p><p>Two records describe the same event and give different dates. A search system can retrieve both. A reader needs to see the passage behind each date and understand why the discrepancy remains.</p><p>The systems series follows the problem from source documents through retrieval, software checks, and human review. Start with MetaVis to see how I evaluated a working prototype. The preliminary doctoral proposal and reading map lay out what I still want to test. <a href="/research/systems/">Browse the systems series</a> · <a href="/research/systems/metavis-evidence-and-evaluation">Read the MetaVis case study</a> · <a href="/research/systems/dissertation-proposal">Open the proposal</a> · <a href="/research/systems/literature-and-faculty">Explore the reading map</a></p><h2 id="how-do-we-work-with-ai-responsibly" tabindex="-1">How do we work with AI responsibly? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-we-work-with-ai-responsibly" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How do we work with AI responsibly?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An assistant that can use tools can turn a misunderstanding into a real change. I want to know which actions a person should review, what evidence the assistant should preserve, and how to correct a mistake. <a href="/life-tracker">Life Tracker</a> describes how I record and check actions in a small personal workflow. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/ordo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ordo</a> documents a broader system for human-directed AI work.</p><p>An earlier project proposal, EverydayAI Newark, asked how people outside the university could practice these skills on real projects. <a href="/about">My background and community work</a></p><h2 id="research-collaborations" tabindex="-1">Research collaborations <a class="header-anchor" href="#research-collaborations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Research collaborations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>I’d welcome a colleague who wants to compare two versions of an AI-assisted assignment or study how reviewers trace a disputed claim. What is one decision we could observe, and what evidence would change our interpretation? <a href="https://people.njit.edu/profile/kwilliam" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Contact me at NJIT about a study</a></p><p>For an independent consulting conversation about a workflow your team is running, tell me where a reviewer has to stop and check the output. <a href="/about#lets-compare-notes">Get in touch about a workflow</a></p>',18)])])}const m=a(s,[["render",i]]);export{w as __pageData,m as default};
